    1: #!/bin/bash
    2: # GATE 13 -- PowerPC single-precision conversion fidelity, on real guest instructions.
    3: #
    4: # What this protects
    5: # -----------------
    6: # #304 gave `frsp` the rounding mode it never read (the narrowing was a host C cast,
    7: # nearest-always, so the three directed modes were unreachable) and taught `stfs` the
    8: # ISA's denormalization band, which it had been flushing to signed zero. #305 stopped
    9: # `lfs`/`stfs` from destroying a NaN's sign and payload: the value-domain transport
   10: # (interpret -> host double -> store) structurally cannot carry them, so the NaN classes
   11: # are handled by bit surgery on the pattern instead.
   12: #
   13: # Why this gate had to exist BEFORE the fixes
   14: # ------------------------------------------
   15: # There is no PowerPC OS rig -- no guest boots on this path -- so the defects were found
   16: # by a cold-debugger spike and every one of them could only be believed once an
   17: # instrument measured it. The rows below were run against the COMMITTED build first and
   18: # their committed bytes recorded; the fix then had to flip exactly the DISC rows and
   19: # leave every PIN untouched. That baseline sweep is what makes "the fix worked" a
   20: # measurement rather than a claim, and it caught three of the author's own wrong
   21: # predictions before any code changed (frsp's RN overflow to Infinity is ISA-correct,
   22: # and the negative band row keeps its sign through the flush).
   23: #
   24: # Three row classes, and why PINs are not decoration
   25: # -------------------------------------------------
   26: #   PIN  -- correct today, must stay. Includes two POLICY pins: for finite values at
   27: #           and above ~2^129 the letter of Book I would splice-WRAP the exponent, and
   28: #           this fork deliberately keeps #287's +/-Inf instead (the letter turns a
   29: #           finite overflow into a NaN pattern: 1.5*2^128 -> 0x7FC00000). A policy pin
   30: #           failing means someone changed a known divergence without updating the
   31: #           record -- worth knowing either way.
   32: #   DISC -- discriminator: committed byte is the defect, want flips with the fix.
   33: #   DIV  -- known divergence left to a later round, pinned so it cannot drift.
   34: #
   35: # #310's eighteen rows cover the eight float UPDATE forms, every one of which
   36: # stopped the emulator before this round -- neither the primary opcodes
   37: # 0x31/0x33/0x35/0x37 nor the indexed 567/631/695/759 were defined or decoded.
   38: # Each form gets BOTH halves of its contract: a value row and a base-register
   39: # row. A diff-review seat caught the first version giving five forms a base row
   40: # only, which would have passed an implementation with the wrong transfer size
   41: # that still advanced the base.
   42: #
   43: # The two non-update rows assert the mirror image, that the base is UNCHANGED --
   44: # and their displacement and index are NONZERO on purpose. With a zero offset
   45: # the effective address equals the base, so a wrongly-updating implementation
   46: # would write back exactly the value the row expects: the same seat found both
   47: # controls vacuous that way.
   48: #
   49: # The control row is load-bearing: `msr=0x2000` must take or every FP instruction
   50: # raises FPU-unavailable and the probe measures nothing. A dead probe SKIPs; it never
   51: # passes quietly. The mode rows additionally read FPSCR back, because a mode that
   52: # silently failed to take would turn every directed-mode row into a second copy of
   53: # the RN row.
   54: #
   55: # Those rows set FPSCR by DEBUGGER WRITE rather than through the guest's own `mtfsf`,
   56: # which used to be necessary: #324 found that instruction building its write mask with
   57: # an eight-bit stride across four-bit fields, so half the FM bits wrote nothing and the
   58: # rest wrote the wrong fields. It is fixed and has six rows of its own now. The
   59: # debugger write stays because it keeps the mode rows independent of a second
   60: # instruction's correctness -- which is the property that let #324 be found at all.
   61: #
   62: # The `mtfsf` rows used to record a DIVERGENCE here: FM=0x80 selects field 0, whose bits
   63: # 1 and 2 are FEX and VX, and mtfsf copied them straight out of frB although Book I
   64: # lists it among the five instructions that "cannot alter FPSCR FEX / VX explicitly".
   65: # #327 closed that, and the row now expects hardware's 90000000 instead of the
   66: # emulator's old f0000000. THREE MORE ROWS MOVED WITH IT, because VX and FEX are
   67: # DERIVED and the recompute re-establishes them from whatever mtfsf did write: FM=0x40
   68: # gains VX from VXSNAN, FM=0x0f gains both from VXSOFT/VXSQRT/VXCVI plus VE, and
   69: # `mtfsf clears` keeps both because its preloaded causes and enables survive the
   70: # field-0 write. FM=0xff is unchanged only by coincidence -- with every cause and
   71: # enable set, derivation reproduces the copy -- and FM=0x01 selects a field with no
   72: # causes in it at all.
   73: #
   74: # All four bytes were PRE-REGISTERED from Book I before the code was written, which is
   75: # what makes this gate the fix's acceptance test rather than a transcript of it.
   76: # FM=0x40 stays the clean companion row for field PLACEMENT: it holds VXSNAN, which
   77: # the guest could not clear at all until #324 (its nibble landed above bit 31).
   78: #
   79: # #325's `fctiwz` rows carry a warning of their own. That row was pinned as a known
   80: # DIVERGENCE from #304 onward with its answer recorded as 00000000 -- and it was never
   81: # measuring the instruction it named: its word had frB = 0, so it converted f0 while
   82: # the probe seeded f1, and the zero came from an empty register. The `fctiwz 1.0
   83: # control` row exists because of it: a wrong register field yields zero for ANY
   84: # operand, so rows expecting 0x80000000 or 0 can be satisfied by accident and a row
   85: # expecting 1 cannot.
   86: set -u
   87: cd "$(dirname "$0")"
   88: . ./lib.sh
   89: 
   90: gate_begin "gate 13: PowerPC FP conversion + FPSCR (#304/#305/#324-#330)"
   91: 
   92: PMAX=${PMAX:-$ROOT/build/gxemul}
   93: LOG=$LOGDIR/gate_ppc.log
   94: 
   95: need_exec "$PMAX"
   96: need_file "$IMAGES/netbsd401-macppc-GENERIC"
   97: command -v python3 >/dev/null || gate_skip "python3 not available"
   98: 
   99: note "binary : $PMAX"
  100: note "machine: macppc/G4, NetBSD kernel loaded so the machine constructs; never executed"
  101: note "cold debugger; big-endian (dump renders value order); msr=0x2000 required for FP"
  102: 
  103: python3 ppc_rounding_probe.py "$PMAX" "$IMAGES" > "$LOG" 2>&1 || true
  104: 
  105: if ! grep -q "PPC_CONV_RESULT=" "$LOG"; then
  106:     note "probe produced no result line; last lines follow"
  107:     tail -5 "$LOG" | sed 's/^/       /'
  108:     gate_skip "probe did not complete"
  109: fi
  110: 
  111: grep -E " ok$| FAIL$" "$LOG" | sed 's/^/       /'
  112: 
  113: # A probe whose control row failed has measured NOTHING, and no other row may be
  114: # believed -- this harness has been bitten by a probe that reported a defect off
  115: # sentinel reads while its guest code had never run.
  116: ctrl=$(grep -o "PPC_CONV_CONTROL=[A-Z]*" "$LOG" | tail -1 | cut -d= -f2)
  117: check "control rows prove the probe measures" "${ctrl:-missing}" "OK"
  118: 
  119: # The rounding-mode WRITE channel is proven once per mode, in its own sessions, by
  120: # reading FPSCR back through the guest's own mffs. Both numbers are asserted, because
  121: # "0 bad" is also what a probe reports when it never read FPSCR at all: the first
  122: # version parsed the debugger's plain `reg`, which does not print FPSCR on this CPU,
  123: # so the check passed vacuously on every run until a diff-review seat predicted the
  124: # hole and the rewrite measured it (43 rows of "unparsed").
  125: modebad=$(grep -o "PPC_CONV_MODEWRITES_BAD=[0-9]*" "$LOG" | tail -1 | cut -d= -f2)
  126: modereads=$(grep -o "PPC_CONV_MODEREADS=[0-9]*" "$LOG" | tail -1 | cut -d= -f2)
  127: check "every rounding-mode write took"    "${modebad:-missing}"   "0"
  128: check "all four modes read back by guest" "${modereads:-0}"       "4"
  129: 
  130: res=$(grep -o "PPC_CONV_RESULT=[0-9]*/[0-9]*" "$LOG" | tail -1 | cut -d= -f2)
  131: got=${res%/*}; want=${res#*/}
  132: check "rows run"     "$want" 138
  133: check "rows correct" "$got"  "$want"
  134: 
  135: # The table must keep enough of each class to be worth running: a gate whose rows are
  136: # all pins cannot detect a reverted fix, and one with no pins cannot detect collateral
  137: # damage. Both counts are asserted rather than trusted.
  138: disc=$(grep -c " DISC " "$LOG")
  139: pins=$(grep -c " PIN " "$LOG")
  140: # Both floors are set to the ACTUAL counts, not below them. They had drifted to 47/26
  141: # against real counts of 50/36, which means a whole class could have lost rows without
  142: # the floor noticing -- the same "a check that cannot fail" species this gate's header
  143: # warns about twice. The two numbers must also sum to the asserted `rows run` above
  144: # (94 + 44 = 138); if they stop summing, a row lost its class rather than the table
  145: # losing a row.
  146: # #335 added two DISC (fmadd/fmsub fusion) and one PIN (the 2by3+1 control that
  147: # proves the hand-assembled A-form register fields land where intended).
  148: check_min "discriminating rows present" "$disc" 94
  149: check_min "pinned rows present"         "$pins" 44
  150: 
  151: # The one row that records a divergence this round deliberately does NOT fix.
  152: div=$(grep -c " DIV " "$LOG")
  153: # #325 retired the one DIV row this gate carried: fctiwz of a NaN answered zero,
  154: # and now answers the most negative value the ISA owes. The count is asserted at
  155: # zero rather than the check being deleted -- a gate that stops counting a class
  156: # stops noticing when a member of it reappears.
  157: check "divergence rows recorded" "$div" 0
  158: 
  159: # Named rows, one per contract, so a single site reverting cannot hide behind a total.
  160: #
  161: # The pattern demands TWO spaces after the name, not one: the probe pads names to a
  162: # fixed column, so a name that is a PREFIX of another ("frsp qNaN" of "frsp qNaN
  163: # payload") matched both rows and the check counted 2 where it wanted 1. A check that
  164: # counts the wrong rows is the same species as a check that cannot fail -- it was
  165: # passing for the wrong reason the moment the table grew a longer name.
  166: for v in "frsp qNaN" "frsp sNaN" "frsp 1+3ulp/2 RZ" "frsp band RP" "frsp band- RM" \
  167:          "frsp tiny RP" "frsp 2^128 RZ" "frsp carry-up RZ" \
  168:          "stfs 2^-127" "stfs band tail" "stfs band-" "stfsx 2^-127" \
  169:          "stfs qNaN payload" "stfs sNaN passthrough" "stfsx qNaN payload" \
  170:          "lfs qNaN-" "composed frsp->stfs NaN" "lfsx qNaN-" "VXSNAN sticky" \
  171:          "fctiwz qNaN" "fctiwz -qNaN" "fctiwz 1.0 control" \
  172:          "fctiwz +Inf" "fctiwz -Inf" "fctiwz 2^31" "fctiwz -(2^31+1)" \
  173:          "fctiwz 1.9 RTZ" "fctiwz -1.9 RTZ" \
  174:          "VXISI fadd Inf-Inf" "VXIDI fdiv Inf/Inf" "VXZDZ fdiv 0/0" \
  175:          "VXIMZ fmul Inf-by-0" "ZX fdiv 1/0" "VXSNAN fadd sNaN" \
  176:          "VXSNAN fcmpu sNaN" "VXCVI fctiwz sNaN" "VXCVI fctiwz qNaN" \
  177:          "clean fadd qNaN" "clean fadd Inf+Inf" "clean fdiv Inf/0" \
  178:          "mtfsf FM=0xff" "mtfsf FM=0x80" "mtfsf FM=0x01" "mtfsf FM=0x0f" \
  179:          "mtfsf FM=0x40" "mtfsf clears" \
  180:          "VX falls on last clear" "VX rises on cause" \
  181:          "FEX rises on enable" "FEX falls with its cause" \
  182:          "FEX order: enable first" "FEX order: cause first" \
  183:          "FX latches once only" \
  184:          "lfsu value" "lfsu updates r3" "lfdu value" "lfdu updates r3" \
  185:          "stfsu value" "stfsu updates r3" "stfdu value" "stfdu updates r3" \
  186:          "fmadd fused 1+-2^-52" "fmsub fused 1+-2^-52" "fmadd 2by3+1 control" \
  187:          "VXIMZ+VXSNAN fmadd" "VXIMZ fmadd Inf-by-0" "VXSNAN fmadd sNaN" \
  188:          "VXISI fmadd Inf+-Inf" "clean fmadd Inf+Inf" \
  189:          "VXISI fmsub Inf-Inf" "clean fmsub Inf--Inf" "clean fmadd 2by3+1" \
  190:          "VXIMZ fmadd 0-by-Inf" "VXSNAN fmadd frC" "VXIMZ fmadd qNaN addend" \
  191:          "VXIMZ not VXISI" "VXISI fmadd neg product" \
  192:          "fnmadd 2by3+1 = -7" "fnmsub 2by3-1 = -5" \
  193:          "fadd RZ mode ignored" "fadd RN control" \
  194:          "lfsux value" "lfsux updates r3" "lfdux value" "lfdux updates r3" \
  195:          "stfsux value" "stfsux updates r3" "stfdux value" \
  196:          "stfdux updates r3" "lfs leaves r3" "lfsx leaves r3"; do
  197:     n=$(count "$LOG" "^$v  .*ok$")
  198:     check "  row: $v" "$n" 1
  199: done
  200: 
  201: # The pins that record a DELIBERATE divergence from the letter of the ISA.
  202: for v in "stfs 2^129 policy" "stfs 3\*2^128 policy" "frsp 2^128 RN"; do
  203:     n=$(count "$LOG" "^$v .*ok$")
  204:     check "  policy pin: $v" "$n" 1
  205: done
  206: 
  207: gate_end

